I grew up in the suburbs of Chicago  which was a wonderful liberal bubble  then I moved to Tennessee for college  after that I moved to Texas for my first  job I met people who grew up shooting  guns with their grandfathers people who  had strong religious convictions about  abortion and gay marriage if I'm being  totally honest the whole thing stressed  me out  I knew we disagreed about so many things  but I didn't want to bring them up I was  worried I might offend someone that I  might get labeled as intense or that I  might end up in one of those politically  correct agree to disagree situations  that everyone hates and I don't think  I'm alone for most of us talking about  politics is stressful and awkward and if  it's not it's probably because you're  only talking to people who agree with  you so what I want to do today is talk  about talking about politics why we  don't why we should and most importantly  how we can do it better so why don't we  like talking about politics for one  thing we're taught from a very young age  that the topic is just rude right just  like money or religion I'm not supposed  to know your salary question your belief  in God or ask who you voted for and then  even when we do start to talk about  politics we usually don't think we're  gonna change anybody's mind it's not  that often that we convince somebody to  change a really deep-seated political  view even more fundamentally when we  talk about politics we often feel  insecure and defensive have you ever  worried that so  might think you're uninformed that you  might get judged for a controversial  opinion research shows that when people  are shown information that contradicts  their political views the parts of their  brain that light up are those associated  with personal identity emotion threat  response in other words we feel insecure  and defensive because we're literally  wired to view attacks on our political  beliefs as attacks on ourselves so we're  conditioned to believe the topic is  Reard we don't think we're gonna change  anybody's mind and we feel insecure and  defensive so then why should we talk  about politics isn't it easier to just  leave it to the Talking Heads on CNN I  actually think that talking about  politics  sharpens our thinking when I spoke to my  conservative friends in Tennessee in  Texas I realized that a lot of things I  think the government should do actually  cost a whole lot of money sharing our  beliefs helps us challenge crystallize  reassess what we believe we start  recognizing trade-offs and our views  become more nuanced and hey if anyone is  going to change your mind about  something it's probably not going to be  Anderson Cooper  despite that persuasive jawline I wish I  had it's going to be your friends  neighbors co-workers classmates people  you trust and that you can have an  actual conversation with even more  importantly though talking about  politics helps us share ourselves with  the world we aren't born with any set of  political beliefs the issues we care  most about come from our most meaningful  experiences for me I think about the day  a close friend in high school came out  to me that was the day that gay rights  became  personal I think about the incredible  public school teachers who are literally  the reason I'm at Stanford today why I  care so much about investing in public  education I think about my parents who  came to this country from India with  nothing in their pockets to give their  children to give me a better life it's  why I think immigrants should be  celebrated not vilified this is the  stuff that makes you you  in the real you the you that is human in  the most beautiful and authentic and  wonderful of ways is too valuable in too  precious to leave locked up inside so we  need to talk about politics more and we  need to talk about politics better and  so here are the five most practical  things I can tell you about how to do  that first come from a place of  curiosity not offense or defense I know  that sounds like Tom Brady started  reading fortune cookies so let me give  you an example when I was in college I  moderated a debate between the College  Democrats and the College Republicans it  actually went really well and so  naturally I was walking around  afterwards being like move over Tom  Brokaw because there is a new face of  NBC but the woman who was organizing the  debate ran up to me furious she made me  watch the video when the Democrat was  speaking I was nodding and smiling  uncontrollably I literally looked like a  bobble head when the Republican was  speaking I just looked angry  I was not coming from a place of  curiosity I was coming from a place of  offense and defense  I just wanted my team to win and as a  result missed out on the opportunity to  learn something second pick the right  time in place discussions about politics  can be really difficult when there is an  audience watching whether that's a real  audience around a dinner table or a  virtual audience on Facebook try waiting  for a more intimate opportunity to talk  to someone like over an over a cup of  coffee as one mediation expert puts it  don't always stand up to those who  different beliefs instead try sitting  down with them three focus on one topic  at a time don't attack the entire  democratic or Republican Party or  everything the person has believed in  their entire lives that's when we feel  most defensive and insecure by focusing  on one topic we can actually accomplish  something over the course of a single  conversation fourth listen actively use  body language that shows you're paying  attention ask questions paraphrase to  verify you understand what they're  talking about most people need to feel  like they've been heard before they'll  listen to someone else or consider  changing their minds fifth and finally  avoid verbal attacks and obviously don't  hit anybody even if we're not usually  ones to lose control it's shocking how  quickly our language can change when  we're talking about things we really  care about language that's zero-sum or  that frames things in terms of good and  evil can kill productive dialogue so  let's review come from a place of  curiosity pick the right time and place  focus on one topic at a time listen  actively  and avoid attacks I know that's actually  a lot to remember I don't have a cute  acronym for you to take home and hold  your gasps but I broke the magic rule of  three but that's kind of the point of  course it's not going to be easy we're  talking about unlearning what we've been  taught our entire lives rewiring a part  of our brain and just like always  talking about politics is sometimes  going to be stressful awkward you name  it but I'll tell you something the  conversations I had over six years of  living in the south have made me a  better friend a better leader and a  better American so let's stop taking the  easy way out let's embrace discomfort  and let's start talking about the things  that really matter  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

